## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of cache-like structures, address translation, and replacement policies at low level. 

## Key Concepts
- TLB structure and page number tagging
- Linear probing and wraparound
- LRU replacement and correctness in a small cache
- Interaction with two-level page tables during misses

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint32_t tag; uint32_t pa; uint8_t valid; uint8_t lru; } tlb_entry;
static tlb_entry tlb[256];
void tlb_init() { for (int i=0;i<256;i++) tlb[i].valid=0; }
bool tlb_lookup(uint32_t va, uint32_t* pa){ uint32_t page = va >> 12; uint32_t idx = page & 0xFF; for(int i=0;i<256;i++){ tlb_entry* e = &tlb[(idx+i)&0xFF]; if(!e->valid) continue; if(e->tag==page){ *pa = e->pa; e->lru = 0; return true; } e->lru++; } return false; }
void tlb_insert(uint32_t va, uint32_t pa){ uint32_t page = va >> 12; uint32_t idx = page & 0xFF; int miss_pos = -1; uint8_t oldest = 0; for(int i=0;i<256;i++){ tlb_entry* e = &tlb[(idx+i)&0xFF]; if(!e->valid){ miss_pos = (idx+i)&0xFF; break; } if(e->lru > oldest){ oldest = e->lru; miss_pos = (idx+i)&0xFF; } }
 tlb[miss_pos].tag = page; tlb[miss_pos].pa = pa; tlb[miss_pos].valid = 1; tlb[miss_pos].lru = 0; }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to multi-core without races?
- What trade-offs arise if you instead make it a set-associative cache?

## Why This Is Asked\n\nTests understanding of inter-thread synchronization without kernel primitives, and memory ordering.\\n\n## Key Concepts\n- Atomic operations, memory_order semantics\\n- Barrier synchronization, generation counters\\n- Spin-wait considerations and pause\n\n## Code Example\n```c
typedef struct {
  atomic_int count;
  atomic_uint64_t gen;
  int N;
} barrier_t;

void barrier_enter(barrier_t* b, int id) {
  uint64_t g = atomic_load_explicit(&b->gen, memory_order_relaxed);
  if (atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&b->count, 1, memory_order_acq_rel) == b->N - 1) {
    atomic_store_explicit(&b->count, 0, memory_order_relaxed);
    atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&b->gen, 1, memory_order_release);
  } else {
    while (atomic_load_explicit(&b->gen, memory_order_acquire) == g) {
      _mm_pause();
    }
  }
}
```
\n## Follow-up Questions\n- How would you ensure fairness if threads stall? \n- How would you extend for multiple, consecutive barrier phases?\n

## Why This Is Asked
Tests familiarity with low-level synchronization and practical debugging.

## Key Concepts
- Atomic primitives (atomic_flag, atomic_fetch_add_explicit)
- Memory ordering (acquire/release)
- Busy-wait optimization (pause)
- Contention and false sharing
- When to prefer futex or mutex

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <immintrin.h>

typedef struct { atomic_flag lock; } spinlock_t;

void spinlock_init(spinlock_t *s){ atomic_flag_clear(&s->lock); }

void spinlock_acquire(spinlock_t *s){
  while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&s->lock, memory_order_acquire)){
    _mm_pause();
  }
}

void spinlock_release(spinlock_t *s){
  atomic_flag_clear_explicit(&s->lock, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement exponential backoff to reduce bus traffic?
- What profiling steps would you perform to detect false sharing?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests low-level memory management, lock-free data structures, and cache-conscious design in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- Thread-local caching minimizes contention
- Central cache enables cross-thread reuse
- ABA avoidance with tagged CAS
- Cache-line alignment/padding to prevent false sharing
- Memory ordering (acquire/release, fences)
- Safe cross-thread deallocation paths

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: allocate
function alloc(): Object {
  if (threadLocal.freeList != null) return threadLocal.freeList.pop()
  refillFromCentral()
  return threadLocal.freeList.pop()
}

function free(obj): void {
  if (belongsToCurrentThread(obj)) threadLocal.freeList.push(obj)
  else centralCache.push(obj)
}
```

