## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of a practical, low-level data structure and its wrap-around behavior, plus efficient empty/full checks in a simple producer/consumer path.

## Key Concepts

- Ring buffers
- Power-of-two masking
- Empty/full checks with head/tail without extra state
- SPSC memory ordering

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  size_t head, tail;
  unsigned char data[N];
} Ring;

int enqueue(Ring *r, unsigned char value) {
  if ((r->head - r->tail) == N) return 0; // full
  r->data[r->head & (N - 1)] = value;
  r->head++;
  return 1;
}
int dequeue(Ring *r, unsigned char *out) {
  if (r->head == r->tail) return 0; // empty
  *out = r->data[r->tail & (N - 1)];
  r->tail++;
  return 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify for multi-producer/multi-consumer?
- What memory-ordering considerations arise on weakly-ordered architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests crash-consistency with persistent memory, memory ordering, and minimal synchronization in a high-throughput queue. Emphasizes per-slot sequencing, proper flush order, and recovery logic after power loss.

## Key Concepts
- Persistent memory semantics and crash-consistency
- Per-slot sequence counters to avoid ABA
- Flush/fence strategies (pmem_persist, sfence, clflushopt)
- Recovery protocol after crash or reset

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  uint8_t payload[16];
  uint64_t seq;
} slot_t;

typedef struct {
  slot_t *slots;
  uint64_t head; // producer position (mod N)
  uint64_t tail; // consumer position (mod N)
  uint64_t mask; // N-1
} pmem_queue_t;

int enq(pmem_queue_t *q, const void *data);
int deq(pmem_queue_t *q, void *out);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multi-producer/multi-consumer while preserving crash-consistency?
- How would you adapt the design for different PMEM persistence APIs and cache-line sizes?
